Chronic Fatigue Syndrome (ME/CFS)
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Profound fatigue >6 months, Post-exertional malaise, Unrefreshing sleep, Normal comprehensive medical workup

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-006
Reason for Visit: Acute functional collapse and severe orthostatic intolerance rendering the patient bedbound and unable to self-hydrate.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a sudden, severe physiological crash with orthostatic presyncope and inability to perform basic activities of daily living. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to rule out acute cardiopulmonary decompensation, shock, or severe acute infection, and to establish safe home-based hydration protocols to prevent impending emergency department transfer prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I tried to do some light gardening two days ago, and now I literally can't lift my head off the pillow without the room spinning and my body aching."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a severe, disproportionate crash (post-exertional malaise) following minor physical exertion 48 hours prior. This occurs on a background of profound, unrelenting generalized fatigue lasting 8 months. The patient experiences entirely unrefreshing sleep, waking up exhausted. They describe severe cognitive impairment ("brain fog") and severe orthostatic intolerance, manifesting as intense dizziness and palpitations upon standing. Widespread, unexplainable muscle pain is present.
Functional Status: Bedbound strictly due to orthostatic intolerance and profound exhaustion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Complete inability to perform basic activities of daily living; relying heavily on spouse for hydration and nutrition, posing an immediate risk of dehydration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Supine, eyes closed, pale, speaking in a whisper. Photophobic.
Physical Exam: Marked tachycardia upon attempting to stand without significant orthostatic hypotension, returning to baseline when supine. Complete absence of focal neurological deficits. Tender cervical lymph nodes palpated bilaterally without frank, massive lymphadenopathy. Generalized mild muscle tenderness without focal joint swelling or severe erythema. Documented history reveals entirely normal comprehensive metabolic panels, autoimmune screens, and infectious serologies.
Environmental Observations: Curtains drawn, room kept completely dark and quiet due to severe sensory sensitivity.
Medication Reconciliation: No current prescription medications; utilizing over-the-counter paracetamol with nil effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Fatigue Syndrome (ME/CFS) - Status: Severe acute exacerbation (PEM crash)
Differentials:

Addison's Disease - Ruled out due to normal skin pigmentation and previously documented normal serum electrolytes and cortisol levels.

Occult Malignancy - Ruled out due to absence of significant weight loss, night sweats, and prior normal comprehensive blood work.

Major Depressive Disorder - Unlikely as primary cause due to prominent orthostatic tachycardia and the classic physical post-exertional physiological crash, rather than isolated psychological anhedonia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in a state of acute functional collapse secondary to a severe post-exertional malaise crash. The profound orthostatic intolerance, rendering the patient unable to stand or self-hydrate, posed an immediate, tangible risk of progressive physiological deterioration and severe dehydration. Urgent after-hours clinical assessment was strictly necessary to definitively exclude acute cardiopulmonary decompensation, severe electrolyte derangement, or acute occult infection via hands-on physical examination. Establishing the absence of an acute medical emergency allowed for immediate clinical reassurance and the implementation of safe, bed-based hydration strategies. This acute inability to mobilize safely out of bed mandated a home visit, satisfying the criteria for an urgent after-hours assessment that could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral rehydration solution (ORS). Advised strict horizontal rest and aggressive energy pacing.
Positive Behavioral Change: When attempting to increase activity in the future, establish a rigid baseline that is strictly 50% of perceived capacity. By stopping activity long before physical exhaustion hits, the patient will avoid the severe physiological penalty of a crash, rewarding the behavior with stabilized baseline energy levels.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to Occupational Therapy for energy conservation and pacing strategies.
DME/Safety (Optional): Shower chair.
Follow-up: Routine GP review via telehealth in 7 days to monitor crash recovery trajectory.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if developing acute chest pain, true witnessed syncope, inability to swallow fluids, or sudden focal neurological weakness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This acute management assumes previously documented normal blood tests remain accurate; sudden changes in symptom character require reassessment.
Clinical Course: ME/CFS is a chronic, fluctuating neuroimmune condition. Severe crashes like this are expected if the biological energy envelope is exceeded, but gradual stabilization is achievable with aggressive, disciplined pacing.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if you actually pass out, develop crushing chest pain, or become completely unable to keep fluids down for more than 12 hours.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your body currently lacks the cellular metabolic capacity to recover normally from exertion. Aggressive horizontal rest and strict electrolyte fluid intake are required to reset your baseline autonomic nervous system.
Actionable Steps: Keep a bottle of oral rehydration fluid immediately next to your bed and take frequent small sips while lying completely flat. Achieving basic hydration without triggering orthostatic stress will stabilize your heart rate, immediately reinforcing the physiological benefit of resting.

